                  \\exp(\tfrac{1}{2} \sum_{x = 1}^k J_{x} A^{-1}_{x y} J_y)
                  \\right)_{\vert J = 0}
                  \\\
                  \&amp; =
                  \\underset{pairings}{\sum}  A^{-1}_{x_{j_1} x_{j_2}} \cdot A^{-1}_{x_{j_3} x_{j_4}} \cdots A^{-1}_{x_{j_{n-1}}x_{j_n}}
                  \\end{aligned}
                  \\,.
          %p
            Here the last
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"} equality
            – known as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wick%27s+theorem"} Wick's theorem
            – comes from simple inspection: take the derivatives inside the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponential+series"} exponential series
            and observe that then the only summands non-vanishing at
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi J
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J = 0
            appears for even
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            and are those where all derivatives hit the monomial
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mrow
                        %mo (
                        %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                          %mfrac
                            %mn 1
                            %mn 2
                        %msubsup
